Z||000035||Fierowrecker||09-11-2001||09:59 PM||||In Grand Rapids Michigan, as soon as the aircraft were ordered to land, Kent County Int, AKA Jerry Ford Int, was swampped by many more planes than they normally see.<BR>The comunities of Grand Rapids, Kentwood, Wyoming, Cascade, etc. opened their arms and hearts to the stranded people on the flights, and donated food and shelter to those in need...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000036||Alex2004||09-11-2001||10:04 PM||||In Oklahoma, eveyone is at gas stations getting gas, all of the Mall's are closed, most college's are being closed, Fire deparments are on high alert. that is all i've heard so far, just thought i'd share.<P>Alex...||||reg||1
Z||000037||fiero56||09-11-2001||11:20 PM||||As I said before, colleges are closed, most of the city is quiet except for people lined up to get gas. There were several stations that ran out, and a friend and I waited an hour and a half to fill up their car. I spoke with an attendant later on tonight, and she said unless they are told different, their prices won't even hit 2.50/gal.<P>While chilling in my apartment there were several exlosion like sounds I could feel, felt like a big sub hitting, rocked the walls, dishes, etc, and it came from the north the direction of the AFB. I thought it was bombs, but turned out it was F-15s breaking the sound barrier as they went out. I've always wondered what it sounded like to hear a sonic boom, was pretty neat.||||reg||1
Z||000038||Cheever3000||09-11-2001||11:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero5:<BR><B>One son finally was able to call his mother at home, who immediatly called the teacher here to let him know his son was oK. The son was running late and got stuck on the subway. If he had been on time, he very well would not be alive now. The second son was IN tower one when the plane hit the building. He grabbed his stuff and his laptop and got out of there. He was able to e-mail his girlfriend at their apartment to have her call his mother who works here.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>That's awesome, and some good news.  Thanks for sharing it.<P>Sonic booms: I heard them daily when I was a little kid growing up near Tulsa.  Got so we barely paid any attention to em.  I last heard one when Chuck Yeager celebrated the first breaking of the sound barrier, in a short flight between Dayton & Cincinnati when I lived there during a major anniversary of the event.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000039||maryjane||09-11-2001||11:57 PM||||War fever running high here. Gas lines 1/4 blk long, gas 1.65, up 35 cents since 3pm.  Goodfellow Air Base here (Intelligence and security traing base, no aircraft) locked down. Armed MPs with dogs on patrol. Just got a email reporting $4 gas in Kansas and $5/gal gas in Kentucky. Grocery stores very busy.||||reg||1
Z||000040||fiero56||09-12-2001||01:59 AM||||Gas lines around here were BLOCKS long. We waited in line for an hour and a half. We needed gas, so we had no choice but to wait. Several stations ran out. <P>I ran around after prices were reposted this Wed morning, and the highest I found after the "hike" was $1.79/gal for regular. Before mid-night it was $1.49/gal. Prices remained constant through the day here, no rise until mid-night. Station attendants said price wasn't expected to rise any more unless someting else comes up.||||reg||1
Z||000041||deceler8||09-12-2001||04:36 PM||||Here in Sioux City, at the airbase, Tomahawk and Sidewinder missiles are being loaded onto F16's which are being flown to unknown destinations.<P>It has been rumored that SAC in Omaha has taken control of our airbase and our fighters are rumored to be heading overseas.<P>The entire military is on Delta alert...the highest alert status.||||reg||1
